WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.140
A monkey receives a drop of juice, with no warning.

2
00:00:08.180 --> 00:00:13.040
Wolfram Schultz's group recorded single dopamine neurons in the midbrain of behaving

3
00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:19.400
monkeys. About three quarters of the recorded neurons, in two monkeys, fired a brief

4
00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:22.620
burst. What is that burst telling the brain?

5
00:00:23.483 --> 00:00:26.703
Now suppose a sound predicts the juice.

6
00:00:27.663 --> 00:00:32.443
After the monkeys learned that the sound predicted the juice, the burst came at the

7
00:00:32.443 --> 00:00:34.383
sound rather than at the juice.

8
00:00:35.103 --> 00:00:37.923
The response moved from the reward to the cue.

9
00:00:38.833 --> 00:00:41.473
Two more results fill in the picture.

10
00:00:42.513 --> 00:00:47.973
When an expected reward was left out, firing dipped below baseline at the time the

11
00:00:47.973 --> 00:00:49.033
reward should have come.

12
00:00:49.893 --> 00:00:53.153
A reward at an unexpected time evoked a burst.

13
00:00:54.093 --> 00:00:58.773
So the firing follows the difference between the reward received and the reward

14
00:00:58.773 --> 00:01:02.193
predicted. That is a reward prediction error.

15
00:01:03.067 --> 00:01:07.887
The simplest rule built on this is the Rescorla-Wagner rule.

16
00:01:08.847 --> 00:01:16.727
Delta is the reward r minus the cue's value V, and the new V is V plus alpha times

17
00:01:16.727 --> 00:01:23.527
delta. With alpha at 0.30 and a reward of 1, V starts at 0.

18
00:01:24.647 --> 00:01:28.427
Trial 1 gives delta of 1.00 and V of 0.30.

19
00:01:28.427 --> 00:01:34.027
Trial 2 gives delta of 0.70 and V of 0.51.

20
00:01:35.067 --> 00:01:39.527
The error at the reward shrinks as the cue's value grows.

21
00:01:40.427 --> 00:01:43.847
After nine trials, V is 0.96.

22
00:01:45.007 --> 00:01:50.887
In the temporal-difference reading, the cue itself arrives unpredicted, so the error

23
00:01:50.887 --> 00:01:57.607
at the cue grows toward V. The burst moves from reward to cue, as in the monkeys.

24
00:01:58.627 --> 00:02:01.407
These are model units, not spikes per second.

25
00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:08.340
Now leave the reward out after long training, with V near 0.95.

26
00:02:09.460 --> 00:02:13.980
Delta is −0.95, the dip at the expected time.

27
00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:18.760
An unpredicted reward, with V at 0, gives +1.

28
00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:23.160
A recording shows the match, but not that the burst teaches.

29
00:02:24.140 --> 00:02:30.400
Keep the rule: in these monkeys, firing follows the reward received minus the reward predicted.
